diagnosis_text:
End-to-end connectivity between racks is currently healthy. Topology-wide reachability tests show pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) can ping pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) and vice versa with 0% loss and ~2–2.5 ms RTT. Control-plane state is also normal: all BGP sessions across the CLOS are Established and exchanging prefixes. For example, leaf_router_0_0 (AS 65200) shows two neighbors up (172.16.0.4, 172.16.0.8) each receiving one prefix; leaf_router_0_1 (AS 65201) similarly has two neighbors up (172.16.0.6, 172.16.0.10). Both spines have three peers up (leaves and super-spine), and the super_spine_router_0 (AS 65000) has both spine peers up, each sending two prefixes. This indicates the inter-rack fabric links and routing are functioning as expected.

The reported “can’t reach at all” symptom is not reproducible now. Notably, all BGP sessions show very recent Up/Down timers (~00:01:38), which suggests a recent control-plane restart or lab reboot that could have caused a brief outage earlier.

Root cause: No persistent fault found; the earlier impact was likely due to a recent FRR/BGP restart or lab reinitialization causing a transient loss of reachability until sessions reestablished.

Corrective fix: No action required at this time. If this was unintended, consider enabling BGP Graceful Restart or BFD to minimize convergence impact, and monitor FRR service health to catch unexpected restarts. === END ===